## What training is

<At levels="simple">

Nobody sets a model's weights by hand. They are found by **training**: show the model examples,
measure how wrong its predictions are, and nudge every weight so it's a little less wrong. Then
repeat, millions of times.

For a language model, any text is full of examples: at every point, the words so far are the
question and the next word is the answer. The counting model learned from the same kind of text
in <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" />, by filling in a table. A network has no table to
fill in, so it starts from random weights and improves them a little at a time.

Getting a program's numbers from examples, instead of writing its rules, is called <GlossaryPopover term="machine-learning">machine learning</GlossaryPopover>.
Doing it with many stacked layers of neurons is <GlossaryPopover term="deep-learning">deep learning</GlossaryPopover>, and a large language model is
a deep network trained on text. All of it is part of artificial intelligence, a field named in
1955.<Cite id="mccarthy1955" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In <ChapterRef to="a-neuron" /> you set the weights yourself. A real model's weights are found by
**training**, a loop with three parts:

1. **Predict.** Run the model on examples whose right answers are known.
2. **Measure.** Score how wrong the predictions were.
3. **Nudge.** Change every weight a little, in whichever direction makes the score better.

Then repeat, millions of times. For a language model the examples come free with any text: at
every point, the words so far are the question and the word that comes next is the answer.

The counting model in <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> learned from text like this too.
Counting is a kind of training: read the text once and fill in a table. A network can't be filled
in like that, because no single weight stands for one pair of words; each weight plays a part in
many predictions. So it starts from random weights and improves them by trial and correction.

This is what <GlossaryPopover term="machine-learning">machine learning</GlossaryPopover> means: a program's numbers come from examples
instead of rules someone wrote. In 1959 Arthur Samuel described a checkers program that got better
by playing, in a paper titled *Some Studies in Machine Learning Using the Game of
Checkers*.<Cite id="samuel1959" /> Networks with many stacked layers of neurons, trained this
way, are <GlossaryPopover term="deep-learning">deep learning</GlossaryPopover>; GPT-2 stacks 12 blocks of them. A large language model is a deep
network trained on text to predict the next token. All of this sits inside the older, wider field
of artificial intelligence, named in a 1955 proposal for a summer workshop at Dartmouth, which
also covers programs that follow rules people wrote, with no learning at all.<Cite id="mccarthy1955" />

</At>

## Start small

<At levels="simple">

To see every weight move, start with a network far smaller than a language model. It has one input
and one output: for each place along a line, it predicts a height. The dots are its examples, and
the curve is its prediction. Press *Take 1 step* to nudge every number once, or *Train* to watch it
learn to follow the dots.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

To see every weight move, start with a network far smaller than a language model. It has one input
and one output: for each place along a line, it predicts a height. The dots are the examples, and
the curve is what the network currently predicts. It has 16 neurons, which squash with tanh, one of
the choices in the neuron above, and 49 numbers, all listed under it. They start random, so the
curve starts wrong. Press *Take 1 step* to see every number nudged once, or *Train* to watch them
learn to follow the dots.

</At>

<CurveFitter />

<At levels="standard deep">

Each step is the loop above: predict a height for every dot, measure how far off the curve is, and
nudge all 49 numbers. The next two sections take apart the measuring and the nudging.

</At>

## Measuring the mistake

<At levels="simple">

To improve, the network needs a score for how wrong it is, called the <GlossaryPopover term="loss">loss</GlossaryPopover>.
For a language model, the loss is high when the model gave the word that really came next a tiny
chance, and low when it gave it a big one. Training means changing the weights so the loss goes
down.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

To improve, the network needs a score for how wrong it is: the <GlossaryPopover term="loss">loss</GlossaryPopover>. For the curve above, the loss is the average
squared distance between the line and the dots. For a language model, it's how surprised the model
was by the word that actually came next: if it gave the right word a high probability, the loss is
low; if it gave it a tiny probability, the loss is high.

Training means changing the weights so that the loss goes down.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The loss, written down

The curve fitter's loss is the **mean squared error** over its $N$ dots:

$$
L = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left(\hat{y}_i - y_i\right)^2
$$

where $y_i$ is dot $i$'s height and $\hat{y}_i$ is the line's height at the same place. A language
model's loss is the **cross-entropy**: the average surprise at each real next token,

$$
L = -\frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} \ln p(x_i \mid x_1, \dots, x_{i-1})
$$

where $p(x_i \mid \dots)$ is the chance the model gave the token that really came next. A chance of
1 costs nothing; a chance near 0 costs a lot. Guessing evenly among $V$ choices gives every one a
chance of $1/V$, so the loss is $\ln V$: that's the dashed line on the tiny model below. Papers
often report **perplexity**, $e^L$: a perplexity of 30 means the model is as unsure as if it were
choosing evenly among 30 tokens.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" />

</At>

## Rolling downhill

Imagine the loss as a landscape, where your position is the setting of every weight and the height
is how wrong the network is. Training is like a ball rolling downhill: find which way is down, take
a small step that way, and repeat. This is <GlossaryPopover term="gradient-descent">gradient descent</GlossaryPopover>. Here it is with a
single weight, so the landscape is just a curve:

<LossLandscape />

Try a large step size: the ball overshoots and bounces around. Try a small one from the start: it
can settle in a dip that isn't the lowest point. Real training balances these. This is what each
*Take 1 step* did to the curve above: every one of its 49 numbers moved a little downhill, and the
▲ and ▼ marks showed which way.

<At levels="simple">

A real network has millions of weights, so its landscape has millions of directions. A method
called **backpropagation** works out which way to nudge every weight at once.<Cite id="rumelhart1986" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

For a network with millions of weights, the landscape has millions of directions. The trick that
makes this practical is **backpropagation**: working backwards from the loss through every
multiply and add, it finds which way to nudge every weight at once.<Cite id="rumelhart1986" /> The
networks on this page use it, written out by hand, and use a popular recipe for the step size called
Adam.<Cite id="kingma2015" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### One step, written down

Gradient descent moves each weight $w$ against the slope of the loss:

$$
w \leftarrow w - \eta \, \frac{\partial L}{\partial w}
$$

where $\frac{\partial L}{\partial w}$ is how fast the loss changes as $w$ changes (the slope in that
direction) and $\eta$ is the step size, also called the learning rate. Backpropagation gets every
slope from the chain rule, working backwards one operation at a time. For a single neuron with
$y = f(z)$ and $z = \sum_i w_i x_i + b$:

$$
\frac{\partial L}{\partial w_i} = \frac{\partial L}{\partial y} \, f'(z) \, x_i
$$

so a weight's slope is the slope arriving from above, times how steep the activation is, times the
input that weight multiplies.<Cite id="rumelhart1986" /> Adam changes the step: it keeps a running
average of each weight's slope and of its square, and divides the first by the square root of the
second, so every weight gets its own step size. The networks here use its usual settings, 0.9 and
0.999 for how much of each average carries over from one step to the next.<Cite id="kingma2015" />

</At>

## Now, a tiny language model

<At levels="simple">

The same recipe trains a language model. The one below reads 3 characters and guesses the next,
using the nursery rhymes from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" />. Each character gets its own
list of numbers, those feed a layer of neurons, and out comes a chance for every character: the
whole path from the last chapter, in miniature. It trains entirely in your browser.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The same recipe trains a language model. The one below reads the 3 characters before a gap and
predicts the next character, using the six nursery rhymes from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" />.
It has every part from the path in <ChapterRef to="a-neuron" />, in miniature:

- each character becomes a vector of 8 numbers, as tokens do in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" />;
- the 3 vectors feed a layer of 64 neurons;
- the neurons feed an output that gives a chance for every character
  (<ChapterRef to="probability-and-sampling" />).

It's a scaled-down version of a design from 2003,<Cite id="bengio2003" /> and it trains entirely in
your browser. All of its numbers start random, the character vectors included, and every step
nudges every one of them.

</At>

<TinyTrainer />

<At levels="simple">

At first it writes random letters. As it trains, its loss falls, and real words appear, then
pieces of real phrases. Its character lists were learned too. That's where the token vectors
of <ChapterRef to="embeddings" /> come from: they are weights like any other.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

At first it writes random letters, and its loss sits on the dashed line: the loss you'd get by
guessing every character with equal chance. As training goes on, the loss falls and the writing
starts to look like the rhymes: real words appear, then fragments of real phrases.

Nothing told it what the characters are. Its character vectors were nudged along with every other
weight, just to make the next character easier to predict. That is the answer to where the token
vectors in <ChapterRef to="embeddings" /> come from: they're weights, learned the same way.

</At>

<At levels="simple">

GPT-2 was trained the same way, just much bigger: it learned from millions of web
pages.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

GPT-2 was trained the same way, just much bigger: it predicted the next token across about 40 GB of
text from 8 million web pages, nudging all of its 124 million weights to make the right tokens more
likely.<Cite id="radford2019" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Dropout

Training GPT-2 also used **dropout**: at each step, some of the numbers flowing through the network
are set to zero at random, each with a chance of 0.1, a different tenth every step.<Cite id="gpt2-config" />
The network can't rely on any one neuron, so it memorizes its training text less and does better on
text it hasn't seen.<Cite id="srivastava2014" /> Dropout is only for training: when the model is used,
nothing is dropped, so the same input always gives the same probabilities.

</At>

## What comes next is built on this

<At levels="simple">

The rest of the course keeps this recipe. Attention and the transformer block, in the next Part,
are more weights, trained the same way. Today's biggest models use the same recipe with far more
weights and text, and a chat assistant is the same model trained further on conversations.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

From here on, every model in the course is weights set by this loop. What changes is how the
weights are arranged, how many there are, and which examples they learn from:

- **Arranged differently.** Attention and the transformer block
  (<ChapterRef to="attention" through="the-transformer-block" />) add new parts to the
  calculation, and training sets their weights just as it set these.
- **Many more of them.** Today's largest models follow the same recipe with billions of weights
  (<ChapterRef to="scale" />), and storing them all takes its own tricks
  (<ChapterRef to="quantization" />).
- **Different examples.** An assistant is a base model trained further on conversations
  (<ChapterRef to="predictor-to-assistant" />).

Training also explains some limits. The loss only rewards text that's likely to come next, not
text that's true, which is one reason models make things up (<ChapterRef to="quirks-and-limits" />).

</At>

<RecapCard>

- A network starts with random weights and learns from examples: predict, measure the mistake,
  nudge every weight, repeat.
- For a language model, any text is examples: the words so far, and the word that came next.
- The loss measures how wrong it is. For a language model: how surprised it was by the real next
  token.
- Gradient descent nudges every weight downhill on the loss; backpropagation works out which way
  that is for every weight at once.
- Token vectors are weights too, learned the same way.

<At levels="deep">

- Each step moves every weight by $-\eta\,\partial L / \partial w$; a language model's loss is the
  average of $-\ln p$ for the real next tokens.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** our tiny model only sees 3 characters back, and <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" />'s model only saw one word.
How much text does a model need to look at to predict well?
